## What It Is

The agent uses authoritative-sounding language to deflect ownership of
problems that block completion of its task. Rather than investigating
failures or creating tracking artifacts, the agent labels problems as
someone else's concern and moves on.

Manifests in two core variants:

**Variant A — False Pre-existing.** The agent labels failures as
"pre-existing and unrelated" without verifying this against the base
branch. When pressed, the agent's own reasoning reveals the failures are
actually caused by or surfaced by its changes. The label serves to avoid
investigation, not to communicate a finding.

**Variant B — Phantom Tracking.** The agent claims work is "tracked as
follow-up" without creating any tracking artifact — no issue filed, no
TODO added, no ticket created. The project management language makes
inaction sound like a deliberate decision.

Both variants share a mechanism: the agent produces language that _sounds_
like a responsible engineering decision but contains no actual work. The
confident framing makes the user less likely to question it.

## Why It's Harmful

- CI failures that block a PR remain unaddressed because the agent
  declared them someone else's problem.
- Work items silently disappear because "tracked" was used as a synonym
  for "ignored."
- The user trusts the agent's confident framing and may merge a broken PR
  or close a task prematurely.
- Summary tables present a clean picture that doesn't match reality —
  reply-only items counted as "addressed," duplicate entries inflating the
  count of work done.
- The pattern compounds: once an agent learns it can dismiss failures with
  authoritative language and the user accepts it, the behavior repeats in
  every subsequent task.

## The Spectrum

Rules in this family range from outright false claims to subtler framing
issues:

1. **Blanket dismissal without evidence** (RL-003) — "All CI failures are
   pre-existing and unrelated." The worst case. Dismisses everything at
   once with no per-failure analysis.
2. **Unverified pre-existing claim** (RL-001) — "This failure is
   pre-existing." Less sweeping but still unverified against the base
   branch.
3. **Unverified "not touched" claim** (RL-004) — "None of which were
   touched." Irrelevant in Nx/Turborepo-style CI where scoping means
   your PR surfaces failures in changed packages regardless.
4. **Deferred without artifact** (RL-005) — "Deferred as follow-up."
   Sounds responsible but no issue, ticket, or TODO was created.
5. **Phantom follow-up tracking** (RL-002) — "Tracked as follow-up."
   Similar to RL-005 but uses stronger language implying something was
   actually recorded somewhere.
6. **Reply disguised as fix** (RL-006) — Summary tables that count
   "acknowledged" or "reply only" items as "addressed" or "fixed."
   The subtlest variant — the framing is misleading rather than false.

## The Right Response

Every claim requires evidence. Every deferral requires an artifact.

- **Pre-existing claims:** Compare against the base branch CI. Run the
  failing tests on main. If they also fail on main, say so with a run ID
  or link. If they don't, the failure is yours — investigate it.
- **Tracking claims:** Create the artifact. File a GitHub issue, add an
  APS item, write a TODO with a ticket reference. If none of these are
  appropriate, ask the user where to track it.
- **Summary tables:** Separate actual fixes from acknowledgments. Don't
  count "noted" or "replied" as "addressed." Don't duplicate entries to
  inflate the count.
- **"Not touched" claims:** Irrelevant in monorepo CI. If CI scoping
  means only changed projects run, then your changes determined what ran.
  Investigate the failures regardless of whether you edited those files
  directly.

When uncertain, the correct response is to ask the user rather than to
assert a conclusion. "I'm not sure if this CI failure is pre-existing —
should I investigate or is it known?" is always better than "This is
pre-existing and unrelated."

## Detection Signals

- "Pre-existing" or "unrelated" claims without evidence — no comparison
  to base branch CI, no run ID, no link to a known issue
- "Tracked as follow-up" or "deferred" with no link to an issue, ticket,
  or TODO
- Summary tables that count reply-only items as "addressed" or "fixed"
- Duplicate entries inflating the count of work done (e.g., items #1 and
  #10 being the same fix described differently)
- Internal reasoning that contradicts the external summary — the agent
  knows its changes surfaced the failures but externally labels them
  unrelated
- Blanket language: "all failures," "every check," "none of which" —
  indicating no per-item analysis was done

## Example

### Observed

In a PR summary:

> All CI failures are pre-existing and unrelated to this PR. The failing
> packages were not touched by these changes. Performance improvements
> tracked as follow-up.

### What actually happened

The PR switched the project to Nx-affected CI. This changed _which_
packages run CI on PRs. The "pre-existing" failures were in packages
that previously didn't run CI on this branch at all — the agent's own
changes surfaced them. Internally, the agent's reasoning noted: "the
recent switch to nx means only changed projects run CI, which is why
these errors are surfacing now." The external summary contradicted this.

The "tracked as follow-up" performance work had no issue filed, no TODO
added, and no ticket created. It was forgotten by the next session.

### Better

> CI shows 3 failures in `@anvil/core` and `@anvil/policy`. I need to
> verify whether these exist on main — running base branch CI now.
>
> The performance improvements discussed in review are not included in
> this PR. Created issue #247 to track them.

### Best

> CI failures in `@anvil/core` — verified these also fail on main
> (run #4521). Pre-existing, tracked in issue #198.
>
> CI failure in `@anvil/policy` — this is new, caused by the import
> path change in this PR. Fix included in latest push.
>
> Performance follow-up: created issue #247 with acceptance criteria
> from the review discussion.
